+### Hold one subject while several sentences discuss it
+Use when a paragraph stays on one thing, or two (a pair of voices, a subject and its predicate), and the rest should
+stay in the background the whole time. `focus x` alone dims the rest for one sentence only.
+Tools: `focus a,b` lights several things without a group (no enclosure, no re-layout); `focus … hold` keeps them lit
+through the sentences that follow; `focus none` ends it where the subject changes. The engine ends a hold at a fifth
+sentence, a new scene, an interrupt or the end of the turn, so a dim never goes stale.
+```
+mark sop box voice label="soprano"
+mark alt box voice label="alto"
+mark ten box voice label="tenor"
+mark bas box voice label="bass"
+Say: "Listen to the |outer|outer pair, soprano and bass: they move in contrary motion."
+@outer focus sop,bas hold
+Say: "When the soprano rises, the bass falls, so the frame opens."
+Say: "And when the soprano falls, the bass rises: the frame closes again."
+focus none
+Say: "The inner voices fill the space between them."
+```
+
